---
title: Heatmaps and Densities
description: Choose quantitative matrix cells, contours, or hexagonal bins to show concentration across two dimensions.
---
NIFT_TANSTACK_DOCUMENT_V1
{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Heatmaps and density charts answer where values concentrate across two\ndimensions. A matrix uses explicit row and column categories or intervals.\nContours summarize a regular scalar grid or an estimated point field; spatial\nbins summarize local observations. Choose the representation that preserves\nthe question instead of defaulting to one color per raw observation."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"choose-the-comparison","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Choose the comparison"}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Reader question"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Start with"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"How does daily activity vary by week and weekday?"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Token use calendar heatmap"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"How many observations fall in each quantitative x-y interval?"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Binned quantitative heatmap"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"What regions of a regular scalar grid cross selected levels?"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Scalar-grid contours"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"What smooth regions enclose similar point density?"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Density contours"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Where are dense clusters while retaining local bin shape?"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Hexagonal bins"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"What value belongs to each pair of named categories?"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"An ordinal cell matrix"}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"../concepts/data-and-channels.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Data and Channels"}]},{"type":"text","value":" explains interval and\ncolor channels. "},{"type":"link","href":"../guides/legends-and-color.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Legends and Color"}]},{"type":"text","value":" covers\ncontinuous color meaning and accessible legend design."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"start-with-a-labeled-matrix","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Start with a labeled matrix"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"An ordinal matrix uses categories on both axes and one quantitative value for\neach cell. Direct labels preserve exact values while color makes broad patterns\nvisible."}]},{"type":"component","name":"live-example","tagName":"md-live-example","attributes":{"id":"labeled-matrix-1"},"properties":{"data-example-group":"labeled-matrix","data-collapsed-indexes":"[1]","data-workspace":"{\"version\":1,\"entry\":\"/src/chart.ts\",\"environment\":\"charts\",\"files\":{\"/src/chart.ts\":\"import { cell, colorGradientLegend, defineChart, text } from '@tanstack/charts'\\nimport { scaleBand } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/band'\\nimport { scaleLinear } from 'd3-scale'\\nimport { quarters, scores, teams } from './data'\\n\\nexport default defineChart({\\n  marks: [\\n    cell(scores, {\\n      x: 'quarter',\\n      y: 'team',\\n      color: 'score',\\n      key: (row) => `${row.team}-${row.quarter}`,\\n      inset: 1,\\n    }),\\n    text(scores, {\\n      x: 'quarter',\\n      y: 'team',\\n      text: (row) => row.score.toFixed(0),\\n      fill: '#0f172a',\\n      fontWeight: 650,\\n    }),\\n  ],\\n  scales: {\\n    x: {\\n      scale: () => scaleBand<string>().domain(quarters).padding(0.04),\\n      axis: { label: 'Quarter' },\\n    },\\n    y: {\\n      scale: () => scaleBand<string>().domain(teams).padding(0.04),\\n      axis: { label: 'Team' },\\n    },\\n  },\\n\\n  color: {\\n    scale: () =>\\n      scaleLinear<string>().domain([60, 100]).range(['#eff6ff', '#60a5fa']),\\n    legend: colorGradientLegend({ label: 'Score', steps: 8 }),\\n  },\\n})\",\"/src/data.ts\":\"export const quarters = ['Q1', 'Q2', 'Q3', 'Q4']\\nexport const teams = ['Core', 'Cloud', 'Mobile']\\n\\nexport const scores = [\\n  { team: 'Core', quarter: 'Q1', score: 72 },\\n  { team: 'Core', quarter: 'Q2', score: 78 },\\n  { team: 'Core', quarter: 'Q3', score: 84 },\\n  { team: 'Core', quarter: 'Q4', score: 88 },\\n  { team: 'Cloud', quarter: 'Q1', score: 66 },\\n  { team: 'Cloud', quarter: 'Q2', score: 74 },\\n  { team: 'Cloud', quarter: 'Q3', score: 81 },\\n  { team: 'Cloud', quarter: 'Q4', score: 86 },\\n  { team: 'Mobile', quarter: 'Q1', score: 82 },\\n  { team: 'Mobile', quarter: 'Q2', score: 79 },\\n  { team: 'Mobile', quarter: 'Q3', score: 91 },\\n  { team: 'Mobile', quarter: 'Q4', score: 94 },\\n]\"}}"},"children":[{"type":"code","value":"import { cell, colorGradientLegend, defineChart, text } from '@tanstack/charts'\nimport { scaleBand } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/band'\nimport { scaleLinear } from 'd3-scale'\nimport { quarters, scores, teams } from './data'\n\nexport default defineChart({\n  marks: [\n    cell(scores, {\n      x: 'quarter',\n      y: 'team',\n      color: 'score',\n      key: (row) => `${row.team}-${row.quarter}`,\n      inset: 1,\n    }),\n    text(scores, {\n      x: 'quarter',\n      y: 'team',\n      text: (row) => row.score.toFixed(0),\n      fill: '#0f172a',\n      fontWeight: 650,\n    }),\n  ],\n  scales: {\n    x: {\n      scale: () => scaleBand<string>().domain(quarters).padding(0.04),\n      axis: { label: 'Quarter' },\n    },\n    y: {\n      scale: () => scaleBand<string>().domain(teams).padding(0.04),\n      axis: { label: 'Team' },\n    },\n  },\n\n  color: {\n    scale: () =>\n      scaleLinear<string>().domain([60, 100]).range(['#eff6ff', '#60a5fa']),\n    legend: colorGradientLegend({ label: 'Score', steps: 8 }),\n  },\n})","lang":"ts","meta":"group=labeled-matrix env=charts file=/src/chart.ts entry","title":"/src/chart.ts","file":"/src/chart.ts"},{"type":"code","value":"export const quarters = ['Q1', 'Q2', 'Q3', 'Q4']\nexport const teams = ['Core', 'Cloud', 'Mobile']\n\nexport const scores = [\n  { team: 'Core', quarter: 'Q1', score: 72 },\n  { team: 'Core', quarter: 'Q2', score: 78 },\n  { team: 'Core', quarter: 'Q3', score: 84 },\n  { team: 'Core', quarter: 'Q4', score: 88 },\n  { team: 'Cloud', quarter: 'Q1', score: 66 },\n  { team: 'Cloud', quarter: 'Q2', score: 74 },\n  { team: 'Cloud', quarter: 'Q3', score: 81 },\n  { team: 'Cloud', quarter: 'Q4', score: 86 },\n  { team: 'Mobile', quarter: 'Q1', score: 82 },\n  { team: 'Mobile', quarter: 'Q2', score: 79 },\n  { team: 'Mobile', quarter: 'Q3', score: 91 },\n  { team: 'Mobile', quarter: 'Q4', score: 94 },\n]","lang":"ts","meta":"group=labeled-matrix file=/src/data.ts collapsed","title":"/src/data.ts","file":"/src/data.ts"}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"bin-events-into-a-calendar","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Bin events into a calendar"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A contribution-style calendar exposes both long-term activity and weekday\nrhythm without drawing one long daily time axis. This example aggregates raw,\nsession-level token events into a complete twelve-month UTC day domain, then\nmaps Sunday weeks to columns and weekdays to rows."}]},{"type":"component","name":"chart-example","attributes":{"id":"118-token-usage-calendar","height":"480"},"children":[]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The example uses "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"binTimeX"},{"type":"text","value":" with D3's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"utcDay"},{"type":"text","value":" interval and an explicit\ntwelve-month domain. Each output row contains the day interval, the summed token\ncount, the session count, and source lineage. Empty bins become real zero-value\ndays and share one consistent neutral treatment."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Calendar placement is a second, explicit step: "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"utcSunday.count"},{"type":"text","value":" produces the\nweek column and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"date.getUTCDay()"},{"type":"text","value":" selects the row. A categorical usage scale\ncreates contribution-style levels, while the accessible chart description and\ncell color explain the zero-to-high usage range. The compact focus tooltip keeps\nthe visible detail to the exact token total and date. Keep all calendar\ncalculations in one time basis—UTC here—to avoid moving events between days\naround daylight-saving transitions."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"aggregate-into-quantitative-cells","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Aggregate into quantitative cells"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A two-dimensional binned heatmap makes density bounded: the number of rendered\ncells depends on the chosen grid, not directly on the number of raw points."}]},{"type":"component","name":"chart-example","attributes":{"id":"24-quantitative-binned-heatmap","height":"480"},"children":[]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Prepare explicit x and y interval endpoints plus the aggregate value. Use\nthresholds that are stable across comparable views and a color domain that\nstates whether absolute count or normalized density is being shown. See\n"},{"type":"link","href":"../concepts/scales-and-d3.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Scales"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for binning and scale ownership."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"trace-levels-through-a-scalar-grid","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Trace levels through a scalar grid"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A scalar-grid contour shows where a sampled field crosses chosen values. The\ninput is a regular row-major grid rather than a set of x/y observations."}]},{"type":"component","name":"chart-example","attributes":{"id":"38-contour-topography","height":"480"},"children":[]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The optional "},{"type":"link","href":"../reference/marks/contour.md","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"contour"},{"type":"text","value":" mark"}]},{"type":"text","value":" accepts the raw\ngrid, dimensions, value channel, and levels. It owns marching-squares topology\nand structured polygons; the chart definition retains the metric and threshold\nchoices. Keep grid orientation and dimensions explicit when changing the\nsample window."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"estimate-point-concentration-with-density-contours","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Estimate point concentration with density contours"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Density contours turn many points into nested level sets. They are useful for\nrevealing cluster shape and overlap when raw dots would occlude one another."}]},{"type":"component","name":"chart-example","attributes":{"id":"39-density-contours","height":"480"},"children":[]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Bandwidth and thresholds change the visible shape. Treat them as analytical\nparameters, keep them stable for comparisons, and explain them when they affect\ninterpretation. The optional\n"},{"type":"link","href":"../reference/marks/density.md","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"densityContour"},{"type":"text","value":" mark"}]},{"type":"text","value":" maps the source channels\nthrough final scales, owns responsive estimation, and emits structured polygons\nand holes without case-owned path construction."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"retain-local-structure-with-hexagonal-bins","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Retain local structure with hexagonal bins"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Hexagonal bins aggregate nearby points in pixel space and encode each bin's\ncount or statistic. They provide a compact alternative when a rectangular grid\nwould impose stronger horizontal and vertical edges."}]},{"type":"component","name":"chart-example","attributes":{"id":"43-hexbin-density","height":"480"},"children":[]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pixel-space binning is responsive work: a changed container changes the spatial\nlayout. The optional "},{"type":"link","href":"../reference/marks/hexbin.md","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"hexbin"},{"type":"text","value":" mark"}]},{"type":"text","value":" owns that\nresolved-layout step, reducer channels, source lineage, and hexagon scene\noutput without a duplicated scale."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"production-checks","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Production checks"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"State whether color represents count, proportion, rate, or another aggregate."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Choose a sequential, diverging, or threshold scale that matches the data\nsemantics."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Keep missing, zero, and out-of-domain cells visually distinct."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Add direct labels only when cells remain large enough to read. A labeled\nordinal matrix is demonstrated in\n"},{"type":"link","href":"../guides/themes-and-styling.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Themes and Styling"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Prefer aggregation over rendering an unbounded raw point layer. See\n"},{"type":"link","href":"../guides/large-data.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Large Data"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]}]}],"headings":[{"id":"choose-the-comparison","text":"Choose the comparison","level":2},{"id":"start-with-a-labeled-matrix","text":"Start with a labeled matrix","level":2},{"id":"bin-events-into-a-calendar","text":"Bin events into a calendar","level":2},{"id":"aggregate-into-quantitative-cells","text":"Aggregate into quantitative cells","level":2},{"id":"trace-levels-through-a-scalar-grid","text":"Trace levels through a scalar grid","level":2},{"id":"estimate-point-concentration-with-density-contours","text":"Estimate point concentration with density contours","level":2},{"id":"retain-local-structure-with-hexagonal-bins","text":"Retain local structure with hexagonal bins","level":2},{"id":"production-checks","text":"Production checks","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\nHeatmaps and density charts answer where values concentrate across two\ndimensions. A matrix uses explicit row and column categories or intervals.\nContours summarize a regular scalar grid or an estimated point field; spatial\nbins summarize local observations. Choose the representation that preserves\nthe question instead of defaulting to one color per raw observation.\n\n## Choose the comparison\n\n| Reader question                                               | Start with                  |\n| ------------------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------- |\n| How does daily activity vary by week and weekday?             | Token use calendar heatmap  |\n| How many observations fall in each quantitative x-y interval? | Binned quantitative heatmap |\n| What regions of a regular scalar grid cross selected levels?  | Scalar-grid contours        |\n| What smooth regions enclose similar point density?            | Density contours            |\n| Where are dense clusters while retaining local bin shape?     | Hexagonal bins              |\n| What value belongs to each pair of named categories?          | An ordinal cell matrix      |\n\n[Data and Channels](../concepts/data-and-channels.md) explains interval and\ncolor channels. [Legends and Color](../guides/legends-and-color.md) covers\ncontinuous color meaning and accessible legend design.\n\n## Start with a labeled matrix\n\nAn ordinal matrix uses categories on both axes and one quantitative value for\neach cell. Direct labels preserve exact values while color makes broad patterns\nvisible.\n\n```ts group=labeled-matrix env=charts file=/src/chart.ts entry\nimport { cell, colorGradientLegend, defineChart, text } from '@tanstack/charts'\nimport { scaleBand } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/band'\nimport { scaleLinear } from 'd3-scale'\nimport { quarters, scores, teams } from './data'\n\nexport default defineChart({\n  marks: [\n    cell(scores, {\n      x: 'quarter',\n      y: 'team',\n      color: 'score',\n      key: (row) => `${row.team}-${row.quarter}`,\n      inset: 1,\n    }),\n    text(scores, {\n      x: 'quarter',\n      y: 'team',\n      text: (row) => row.score.toFixed(0),\n      fill: '#0f172a',\n      fontWeight: 650,\n    }),\n  ],\n  scales: {\n    x: {\n      scale: () => scaleBand<string>().domain(quarters).padding(0.04),\n      axis: { label: 'Quarter' },\n    },\n    y: {\n      scale: () => scaleBand<string>().domain(teams).padding(0.04),\n      axis: { label: 'Team' },\n    },\n  },\n\n  color: {\n    scale: () =>\n      scaleLinear<string>().domain([60, 100]).range(['#eff6ff', '#60a5fa']),\n    legend: colorGradientLegend({ label: 'Score', steps: 8 }),\n  },\n})\n```\n\n```ts group=labeled-matrix file=/src/data.ts collapsed\nexport const quarters = ['Q1', 'Q2', 'Q3', 'Q4']\nexport const teams = ['Core', 'Cloud', 'Mobile']\n\nexport const scores = [\n  { team: 'Core', quarter: 'Q1', score: 72 },\n  { team: 'Core', quarter: 'Q2', score: 78 },\n  { team: 'Core', quarter: 'Q3', score: 84 },\n  { team: 'Core', quarter: 'Q4', score: 88 },\n  { team: 'Cloud', quarter: 'Q1', score: 66 },\n  { team: 'Cloud', quarter: 'Q2', score: 74 },\n  { team: 'Cloud', quarter: 'Q3', score: 81 },\n  { team: 'Cloud', quarter: 'Q4', score: 86 },\n  { team: 'Mobile', quarter: 'Q1', score: 82 },\n  { team: 'Mobile', quarter: 'Q2', score: 79 },\n  { team: 'Mobile', quarter: 'Q3', score: 91 },\n  { team: 'Mobile', quarter: 'Q4', score: 94 },\n]\n```\n\n## Bin events into a calendar\n\nA contribution-style calendar exposes both long-term activity and weekday\nrhythm without drawing one long daily time axis. This example aggregates raw,\nsession-level token events into a complete twelve-month UTC day domain, then\nmaps Sunday weeks to columns and weekdays to rows.\n\n<!-- ::chart-example id=118-token-usage-calendar height=480 -->\n\nThe example uses `binTimeX` with D3's `utcDay` interval and an explicit\ntwelve-month domain. Each output row contains the day interval, the summed token\ncount, the session count, and source lineage. Empty bins become real zero-value\ndays and share one consistent neutral treatment.\n\nCalendar placement is a second, explicit step: `utcSunday.count` produces the\nweek column and `date.getUTCDay()` selects the row. A categorical usage scale\ncreates contribution-style levels, while the accessible chart description and\ncell color explain the zero-to-high usage range. The compact focus tooltip keeps\nthe visible detail to the exact token total and date. Keep all calendar\ncalculations in one time basis—UTC here—to avoid moving events between days\naround daylight-saving transitions.\n\n## Aggregate into quantitative cells\n\nA two-dimensional binned heatmap makes density bounded: the number of rendered\ncells depends on the chosen grid, not directly on the number of raw points.\n\n<!-- ::chart-example id=24-quantitative-binned-heatmap height=480 -->\n\nPrepare explicit x and y interval endpoints plus the aggregate value. Use\nthresholds that are stable across comparable views and a color domain that\nstates whether absolute count or normalized density is being shown. See\n[Scales](../concepts/scales-and-d3.md) for binning and scale ownership.\n\n## Trace levels through a scalar grid\n\nA scalar-grid contour shows where a sampled field crosses chosen values. The\ninput is a regular row-major grid rather than a set of x/y observations.\n\n<!-- ::chart-example id=38-contour-topography height=480 -->\n\nThe optional [`contour` mark](../reference/marks/contour.md) accepts the raw\ngrid, dimensions, value channel, and levels. It owns marching-squares topology\nand structured polygons; the chart definition retains the metric and threshold\nchoices. Keep grid orientation and dimensions explicit when changing the\nsample window.\n\n## Estimate point concentration with density contours\n\nDensity contours turn many points into nested level sets. They are useful for\nrevealing cluster shape and overlap when raw dots would occlude one another.\n\n<!-- ::chart-example id=39-density-contours height=480 -->\n\nBandwidth and thresholds change the visible shape. Treat them as analytical\nparameters, keep them stable for comparisons, and explain them when they affect\ninterpretation. The optional\n[`densityContour` mark](../reference/marks/density.md) maps the source channels\nthrough final scales, owns responsive estimation, and emits structured polygons\nand holes without case-owned path construction.\n\n## Retain local structure with hexagonal bins\n\nHexagonal bins aggregate nearby points in pixel space and encode each bin's\ncount or statistic. They provide a compact alternative when a rectangular grid\nwould impose stronger horizontal and vertical edges.\n\n<!-- ::chart-example id=43-hexbin-density height=480 -->\n\nPixel-space binning is responsive work: a changed container changes the spatial\nlayout. The optional [`hexbin` mark](../reference/marks/hexbin.md) owns that\nresolved-layout step, reducer channels, source lineage, and hexagon scene\noutput without a duplicated scale.\n\n## Production checks\n\n- State whether color represents count, proportion, rate, or another aggregate.\n- Choose a sequential, diverging, or threshold scale that matches the data\n  semantics.\n- Keep missing, zero, and out-of-domain cells visually distinct.\n- Add direct labels only when cells remain large enough to read. A labeled\n  ordinal matrix is demonstrated in\n  [Themes and Styling](../guides/themes-and-styling.md).\n- Prefer aggregation over rendering an unbounded raw point layer. See\n  [Large Data](../guides/large-data.md).\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\ntitle: Heatmaps and Densities\ndescription: Choose quantitative matrix cells, contours, or hexagonal bins to show concentration across two dimensions.\n---\n\nHeatmaps and density charts answer where values concentrate across two\ndimensions. A matrix uses explicit row and column categories or intervals.\nContours summarize a regular scalar grid or an estimated point field; spatial\nbins summarize local observations. Choose the representation that preserves\nthe question instead of defaulting to one color per raw observation.\n\n## Choose the comparison\n\n| Reader question                                               | Start with                  |\n| ------------------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------- |\n| How does daily activity vary by week and weekday?             | Token use calendar heatmap  |\n| How many observations fall in each quantitative x-y interval? | Binned quantitative heatmap |\n| What regions of a regular scalar grid cross selected levels?  | Scalar-grid contours        |\n| What smooth regions enclose similar point density?            | Density contours            |\n| Where are dense clusters while retaining local bin shape?     | Hexagonal bins              |\n| What value belongs to each pair of named categories?          | An ordinal cell matrix      |\n\n[Data and Channels](../concepts/data-and-channels.md) explains interval and\ncolor channels. [Legends and Color](../guides/legends-and-color.md) covers\ncontinuous color meaning and accessible legend design.\n\n## Start with a labeled matrix\n\nAn ordinal matrix uses categories on both axes and one quantitative value for\neach cell. Direct labels preserve exact values while color makes broad patterns\nvisible.\n\n```ts group=labeled-matrix env=charts file=/src/chart.ts entry\nimport { cell, colorGradientLegend, defineChart, text } from '@tanstack/charts'\nimport { scaleBand } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/band'\nimport { scaleLinear } from 'd3-scale'\nimport { quarters, scores, teams } from './data'\n\nexport default defineChart({\n  marks: [\n    cell(scores, {\n      x: 'quarter',\n      y: 'team',\n      color: 'score',\n      key: (row) => `${row.team}-${row.quarter}`,\n      inset: 1,\n    }),\n    text(scores, {\n      x: 'quarter',\n      y: 'team',\n      text: (row) => row.score.toFixed(0),\n      fill: '#0f172a',\n      fontWeight: 650,\n    }),\n  ],\n  scales: {\n    x: {\n      scale: () => scaleBand<string>().domain(quarters).padding(0.04),\n      axis: { label: 'Quarter' },\n    },\n    y: {\n      scale: () => scaleBand<string>().domain(teams).padding(0.04),\n      axis: { label: 'Team' },\n    },\n  },\n\n  color: {\n    scale: () =>\n      scaleLinear<string>().domain([60, 100]).range(['#eff6ff', '#60a5fa']),\n    legend: colorGradientLegend({ label: 'Score', steps: 8 }),\n  },\n})\n```\n\n```ts group=labeled-matrix file=/src/data.ts collapsed\nexport const quarters = ['Q1', 'Q2', 'Q3', 'Q4']\nexport const teams = ['Core', 'Cloud', 'Mobile']\n\nexport const scores = [\n  { team: 'Core', quarter: 'Q1', score: 72 },\n  { team: 'Core', quarter: 'Q2', score: 78 },\n  { team: 'Core', quarter: 'Q3', score: 84 },\n  { team: 'Core', quarter: 'Q4', score: 88 },\n  { team: 'Cloud', quarter: 'Q1', score: 66 },\n  { team: 'Cloud', quarter: 'Q2', score: 74 },\n  { team: 'Cloud', quarter: 'Q3', score: 81 },\n  { team: 'Cloud', quarter: 'Q4', score: 86 },\n  { team: 'Mobile', quarter: 'Q1', score: 82 },\n  { team: 'Mobile', quarter: 'Q2', score: 79 },\n  { team: 'Mobile', quarter: 'Q3', score: 91 },\n  { team: 'Mobile', quarter: 'Q4', score: 94 },\n]\n```\n\n## Bin events into a calendar\n\nA contribution-style calendar exposes both long-term activity and weekday\nrhythm without drawing one long daily time axis. This example aggregates raw,\nsession-level token events into a complete twelve-month UTC day domain, then\nmaps Sunday weeks to columns and weekdays to rows.\n\n<!-- ::chart-example id=118-token-usage-calendar height=480 -->\n\nThe example uses `binTimeX` with D3's `utcDay` interval and an explicit\ntwelve-month domain. Each output row contains the day interval, the summed token\ncount, the session count, and source lineage. Empty bins become real zero-value\ndays and share one consistent neutral treatment.\n\nCalendar placement is a second, explicit step: `utcSunday.count` produces the\nweek column and `date.getUTCDay()` selects the row. A categorical usage scale\ncreates contribution-style levels, while the accessible chart description and\ncell color explain the zero-to-high usage range. The compact focus tooltip keeps\nthe visible detail to the exact token total and date. Keep all calendar\ncalculations in one time basis—UTC here—to avoid moving events between days\naround daylight-saving transitions.\n\n## Aggregate into quantitative cells\n\nA two-dimensional binned heatmap makes density bounded: the number of rendered\ncells depends on the chosen grid, not directly on the number of raw points.\n\n<!-- ::chart-example id=24-quantitative-binned-heatmap height=480 -->\n\nPrepare explicit x and y interval endpoints plus the aggregate value. Use\nthresholds that are stable across comparable views and a color domain that\nstates whether absolute count or normalized density is being shown. See\n[Scales](../concepts/scales-and-d3.md) for binning and scale ownership.\n\n## Trace levels through a scalar grid\n\nA scalar-grid contour shows where a sampled field crosses chosen values. The\ninput is a regular row-major grid rather than a set of x/y observations.\n\n<!-- ::chart-example id=38-contour-topography height=480 -->\n\nThe optional [`contour` mark](../reference/marks/contour.md) accepts the raw\ngrid, dimensions, value channel, and levels. It owns marching-squares topology\nand structured polygons; the chart definition retains the metric and threshold\nchoices. Keep grid orientation and dimensions explicit when changing the\nsample window.\n\n## Estimate point concentration with density contours\n\nDensity contours turn many points into nested level sets. They are useful for\nrevealing cluster shape and overlap when raw dots would occlude one another.\n\n<!-- ::chart-example id=39-density-contours height=480 -->\n\nBandwidth and thresholds change the visible shape. Treat them as analytical\nparameters, keep them stable for comparisons, and explain them when they affect\ninterpretation. The optional\n[`densityContour` mark](../reference/marks/density.md) maps the source channels\nthrough final scales, owns responsive estimation, and emits structured polygons\nand holes without case-owned path construction.\n\n## Retain local structure with hexagonal bins\n\nHexagonal bins aggregate nearby points in pixel space and encode each bin's\ncount or statistic. They provide a compact alternative when a rectangular grid\nwould impose stronger horizontal and vertical edges.\n\n<!-- ::chart-example id=43-hexbin-density height=480 -->\n\nPixel-space binning is responsive work: a changed container changes the spatial\nlayout. The optional [`hexbin` mark](../reference/marks/hexbin.md) owns that\nresolved-layout step, reducer channels, source lineage, and hexagon scene\noutput without a duplicated scale.\n\n## Production checks\n\n- State whether color represents count, proportion, rate, or another aggregate.\n- Choose a sequential, diverging, or threshold scale that matches the data\n  semantics.\n- Keep missing, zero, and out-of-domain cells visually distinct.\n- Add direct labels only when cells remain large enough to read. A labeled\n  ordinal matrix is demonstrated in\n  [Themes and Styling](../guides/themes-and-styling.md).\n- Prefer aggregation over rendering an unbounded raw point layer. See\n  [Large Data](../guides/large-data.md).\n"}
